  "response": "**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component correctly implements the core WAI-ARIA APG Toolbar pattern (roving tabindex, native `<button>` elements, arrow key navigation, visible focus indicator). However, it contains a critical state communication gap (`aria-pressed` is hardcoded to `false` and never updates), which breaks assistive technology's understanding of widget state. Additionally, the React focus management design carries a framework-timing risk that could cause focus loss during rapid keyboard interaction. The semantic structure and tab order are sound; these two gaps must be resolved before shipping.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**:\nBefore reading the code, I predicted: (1) `aria-pressed` would be static or misaligned with selection state, (2) React's synchronous `.focus()` call after `setActive` might race with re-renders during rapid arrow key presses, (3) roving tabindex and landmark structure would be correct. Investigation confirmed predictions 1 & 2 as actual design gaps; prediction 3 was accurate.\n\n**Critical Findings** (blocks access):\nNone found. The component does not block core functionality for any user group.\n\n**Major Findings** (significantly degrades experience):\n1. **Static `aria-pressed` breaks state communication to assistive technology**\n   - **Evidence**: `FormatToolbar.tsx:38` \u2014 `aria-pressed={false}` is hardcoded and never updates based on selection or applied formatting state.\n   - **User group impacted**: Screen reader users, keyboard-only users relying on AT feedback.\n   - **Expected behavior**: Per WAI-ARIA APG Toolbar pattern & WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value), `aria-pressed` must dynamically reflect the widget's state. If this is a toggle toolbar, it should update to `true`/`false` based on applied formatting. If it is a command toolbar (click once to apply, no persistent state), `aria-pressed` should be removed entirely.\n   - **Fix**: Either remove `aria-pressed` if the toolbar only issues commands, or sync it dynamically: `aria-pressed={isFormatApplied(name)}`. Ensure the boolean reflects programmatic state, not just focus.\n\n2. **Focus management design risks React re-render timing**\n   - **Evidence**: `FormatToolbar.tsx:18` \u2014 `refs.current[i]?.focus()` is called synchronously inside `handleKeyDown` after `setActive(i)`.\n   - **User group impacted**: Keyboard-only users, screen reader users navigating via arrow keys.\n   - **Expected behavior**: Per WAI-ARIA APG & React best practices, focus should be synced to state changes outside the synchronous event cycle to avoid race conditions where React batches updates before DOM reflects `tabIndex` changes.\n   - **Fix**: Move focus sync to a `useEffect` that watches `active`, or defer with `setTimeout(0)` / `requestAnimationFrame`. Example:\n     ```jsx\n     useEffect(() => {\n       refs.current[active]?.focus();\n     }, [active]);\n     ```\n\n**Minor Findings** (friction but workaround exists):\n- **Missing keyboard shortcut discoverability**: The toolbar implements Home/End navigation but provides no visible or AT-announced hint that these keys are supported. Per WCAG 2.4.1 & APG guidance, document custom key behavior via `aria-describedby` on the toolbar or a tooltip. Add `aria-describedby=\"toolbar-help\"` with a visually hidden `<p id=\"toolbar-help\">Use arrow keys to navigate, Home/End for first/last item.</p>`.\n- **Button labels rely on text content**: If this component is later refactored to use icon-only buttons (common in rich text editors), the current `aria-pressed` and text-based labeling will break. Ensure future iterations add `aria-label` or `title` fallbacks.\n\n**Enhancements** (best practice not met):\n- **Focus indicator contrast verification**: CSS uses `#1a5fb4` outline on assumed white background (~4.6:1). This passes WCAG 2.4.7/2.4.13, but verify against actual page backgrounds in all themes. If dark mode or custom backgrounds are used, recalculate contrast to ensure \u22653:1 change against adjacent colors.\n- **Add `aria-keyshortcuts` for documentation**: Per WAI-ARIA APG, document the arrow key model using `aria-keyshortcuts=\"ArrowLeft ArrowRight Home End\"` on the `[role=\"toolbar\"]` element for AT discoverability.\n\n**What's Missing** (gaps, unhandled edge cases, unstated assumptions):\n- **Dynamic state sync**: No mechanism tracks which formatting is currently applied vs which button has focus. If `onCommand` toggles formatting, the UI must reflect that state back to the buttons.\n- **Escape key handling**: The toolbar does not handle `Escape`. Per APG, pressing Escape should typically blur the active button or reset focus to a default trigger if this toolbar is part of a larger interactive flow.\n- **Disabled state handling**: No support for `disabled` buttons within the roving tabindex model. Disabled items should be skipped during arrow key navigation and not receive focus.\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes**:\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | HIGH | Static `aria-pressed`, missing state sync, undocumented keyboard shortcuts |\n| Keyboard-only | MEDIUM | Roving tabindex works but React timing risk; no Escape handling |\n| Low vision | LOW | Focus indicator passes contrast threshold; layout is simple flex row |\n| Cognitive | LOW | Clear text labels; consistent arrow key behavior; no timeouts or complex flows |\n\n**Verdict Justification**: The component's structural foundation (native buttons, roving tabindex, toolbar role, visible focus) is solid and aligns with the WAI-ARIA APG Toolbar pattern. However, the hardcoded `aria-pressed={false}` creates a silent state communication failure that screen reader users will experience as inconsistent or broken feedback. Combined with the synchronous React focus call that risks focus loss during rapid navigation, the design requires revision before release. Upgrading to ACCEPT requires dynamic state sync for `aria-pressed`, framework-safe focus management, and documentation of custom key behavior.\n\n**Open Questions (unscored)**:\n- Does `onCommand` toggle formatting state or issue one-time commands? This determines whether `aria-pressed` should be removed or dynamically updated.\n- Is this toolbar embedded in a larger interactive context (e.g., rich text editor) where Escape key behavior or focus restoration to the editor viewport is required?\n- Are there theme variants that change button/background colors that could reduce the focus indicator contrast below 3:1?\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: toolbar-static-aria-pressed\nfingerprint: a7f3c9e2b1d4\nsource: src/components/FormatToolbar.tsx:38\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value; WAI-ARIA APG Toolbar Pattern\nsection_508_fpc_context: not in scope\nseverity: MAJOR\nperspective_alarms: screen reader=HIGH, keyboard-only=MEDIUM\nevidence: `aria-pressed={false}` hardcoded on all buttons; never updates based on selection or applied state.\nreproduction_steps: 1. Tab into toolbar. 2. Press ArrowRight to move focus. 3. Listen to AT announcement.\nexpected_behavior: aria-pressed should reflect current formatting state (true/false) or be removed if purely command-based.\nactual_behavior: Always announces \"not pressed\" regardless of focus position or applied formatting.\ntrend: new\n```",
